` THE DANCE. this is a prototype. a ding says that a tune is coming, and then the tune plays. a move of the
` cursor that lands on a beat of it, in any direction, is in time. a move
` that lands between two beats starts the count over. a beat that goes by with no move does not, and a
` beat that is a rest counts the same as one that is a note, so moving on every beat and moving only
` with the notes both work. the reward is in the bubbles of the beat, further down: every move in time
` keeps one, and each one that is kept is worth a step of the bar of the multiplier when the tune is over.
` nothing is written on the screen about any of this, and a move makes no sound. this is what shows:
` THE FLASH. the outline of the cursor flashes white when it is moved during a dance. the closer the move
` was to a beat, the whiter, so a move halfway between two beats does not flash at all.
` A PERFECT MOVE is one within BEAT_PERFECT milliseconds of the beat. its flash is all the way white.
` THE BUBBLES OF THE BEAT. on every beat, a bubble comes up from the bottom of the screen, and drifts up.
` it is for the beat after. if the cursor is moved in time with that beat, the bubble freezes where it
` is. if it has not been by the time that the beat comes, the bubble pops, right on the beat, so that
` the pop shows when the move should have been made. a move that is a little late is still in time,
` and then the bubble that has just started to pop comes back, and freezes. a move that is out of time
` pops the bubble that is drifting up. the ones that are frozen stay, whatever happens after.
` every beat of the dance has a place of its own along the bottom of the screen, from the first one on
` the far left to the last note of the tune on the far right, and the places are evenly spaced, so how
` far apart they are depends on how long the tune is. a bubble comes up in the place of its beat.
` and a bubble only comes up if one of the last BEAT_BUBBLE_RECENT beats had a move in time with it. so
` there are none until the first move that is in time, and they stop if the player stops.
` when the tune is over, the ones that are frozen pop one at a time, from left to right, with the sound
` that a bubble makes when it pops. each one moves the bar of the multiplier up by one, the way that a
` swap that pops fish does. the bar wiggles and fizzes for each one. see wiggleMultiplierBar.
` the ding is two beats ahead of the first note, so the beats can be felt before the tune starts, and
` moves count from the ding on. a quick tune is counted at half speed, and one that is too slow, even
` so, is not a dance at all: it plays with no ding. see beatPulseSteps.
` the tunes that start on their own are dances while a game is being played. with FISH_DEBUG, the M key
` starts one right away, and so does the button in the "Dance" window of the debug panel, which also
` says how far off the beat the last move was. all of it is in fish_routines_beat.
global BEAT_ON = 0 `1 from when a dance is set up until its tune is over
global BEAT_PULSE = 0 `how many milliseconds there are from one beat of the dance to the next
global BEAT_PULSE_STEPS = 1 `and how many beats of the melody that is. 2 for a quick tune.
global BEAT_LAST_MS = 0 `when the last beat was. the first one is the ding.
global BEAT_DING_MS = -1 `when the ding is due, or -1 once it has been heard
global BEAT_HIT_MS = -1 `the beat that the last move in time landed on, so that no beat counts twice
global BEAT_STREAK = 0 `how many moves in a row have been in time. for the debug panel.
global BEAT_LAST_OFF = 0 `how far from the beat the last move was, in milliseconds. less than 0 is early. for the debug panel.
global BEAT_JUDGED_FRAME as long = -1 `the frame that a move was last looked at on, because collectInput can run many times on one
global BEAT_PULSE_MIN = 450 `a beat that is quicker than this is too quick to move to,
global BEAT_PULSE_MAX = 800 `and one that is slower than this is too slow to feel
global BEAT_WINDOW = 95 `a move this many milliseconds from a beat, either side, is in time
global BEAT_LEAD_PULSES = 2 `how many beats ahead of the first note the ding is
global BEAT_PERFECT = 35 `a move this many milliseconds from a beat, either side, is a perfect one
global BEAT_FLASH# = 0 `how white the last move made the outline of the cursor, from 0 to 1
global BEAT_FLASH_AT = 0 `when that move was
global BEAT_FLASHING = 0 `1 while the outline is not back to its own color yet
global BEAT_FLASH_TIME = 320 `how long the flash takes to die away, in milliseconds

` the bubbles of the beat. they are not the bubbles of fish_routines_bubbles, because those cannot stay put.
#constant BEAT_BUBBLE_OFF 0
#constant BEAT_BUBBLE_RISING 1
#constant BEAT_BUBBLE_HANGING 2
#constant BEAT_BUBBLE_POPPING 3
global BEAT_BUBBLE_RECENT = 3 `a bubble only comes up if one of this many beats before the one that it is for had a move in time
global BEAT_BUBBLE_MARGIN = 16 `how far the first and the last place are from the sides of the screen, in pixels
global BEAT_BUBBLE_SLOT = 0 `the place that the next bubble comes up in, counting from 0 on the left
global BEAT_BUBBLE_SLOTS = 1 `how many places there are, which is how many beats the dance has after the ding
global BEAT_BUBBLE_MAX = 64 `when there are this many, a new one takes the place of the oldest
global BEAT_BUBBLE_NEXT = 0 `the one that the next bubble will be
global BEAT_BUBBLE_BEAT_MS = 0 `the last beat that a bubble came up on
global BEAT_BUBBLE_POP_MS = 140 `how long a pop takes
global BEAT_CASH_GAP = 160 `when the tune is over, the ones that are hanging pop this many milliseconds apart
global BEAT_CASH_MS = 0 `when the next one of them pops
global BEAT_POP_VOLUME# = .3 `how loud the pop of each of them is. the pop of any other bubble is about .2.
global BEAT_BUBBLE_RISE_MIN = 45 `a bubble drifts up at somewhere between this many pixels a second
global BEAT_BUBBLE_RISE_MAX = 110 `and this many
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_SPRITE(64)
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_STATE(64) `one of the BEAT_BUBBLE_ numbers above
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_FROM_MS(64) `when it came up, or when it started to pop or to leave
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_DUE_MS(64) `the beat that it is for, which is when it gets to where it hangs
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_X(64) `the place that it came up in, in whole pixels from the left of the screen
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_EXACT_X#(64) `the same, to a part of a pixel, so that the places are exactly as far apart as each other
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_Y#(64) `where it is now
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_SHOW_X#(64) `where it was drawn last, with its wobble. it freezes there.
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_RISE(64) `how fast it drifts up, in pixels a second
global dim BEAT_BUBBLE_SIZE(64)

` THE MUSIC OF THE CORAL. when a song is cast and coral grows, a choir sings one of a few tunes of praise,
` over the brighter of the two chords. when the coral that grows is horror coral, there is a scream from
` far away, and then voices in pain sing one of a few laments, over the darker chord. playCoralMusic, in
` fish_routines_music, does it, and the tunes are at the end of setup_melodies.
global MELODY_CORAL_FIRST = 0 `the first of the tunes of praise
global MELODY_CORAL_COUNT = 0 `and how many there are
global MELODY_HORROR_FIRST = 0 `the same, for the laments
global MELODY_HORROR_COUNT = 0

` no tune is ever heard twice while the game is open. the ones that were written by hand are played
` first, each of them once, in an order that is different every time. once they have all been heard,
` every tune after that is made up on the spot, and one that is made up never comes back.
` makeCoralTune, in fish_routines_music, makes them up, and writes them into the melody MELODY_MADE.
global dim MELODY_HEARD(MELODY_MAX) `1 for every melody of the coral that has been played since the game was opened
global MELODY_MADE = -1 `the melody that the tunes that are made up are written into
global MELODY_MADE_BEATS = 24 `the most beats that one of those can have
global dim CORAL_TUNE_SCALE(10) `the notes that a tune of praise is made up out of. they sound fine over anything.
global dim CORAL_TUNE_ENDS(10) `where a tune can come to rest: six places for praise, and then four for a lament
global CORAL_MUSIC_VOLUME# = 1.6 `how loud the notes of these tunes are, next to the notes of the tunes that play on their own
global CORAL_SCREAM_VOLUME# = .3 `how loud the scream is, from 0 to 1
global CORAL_LAMENT_WAIT = 1500 `the lament holds off for this many milliseconds, so that the scream is heard first
global MELODY_GAIN# = 1 `what the notes of the melody that is playing are turned up or down by

` THE RECORDED PRAISE. the praise is not played on an instrument any more. it is a woman singing, cut into
` phrases from a recording by Tools/coral_clips_gen.py, and when coral grows, one of the phrases is played
` as it is. they take turns in an order that is different every time around, and the one that ended the
` last time around never starts the next. there are only as many as the recording had in it, so one is
` heard again once all of them have been. CORAL_PRAISE_RECORDED set to 0 goes back to the choir and its tunes.
` the laments are still tunes, but the wail that sings them is cut from a recording of a monster now, by
` the same tool, and so are the screams.
global CORAL_PRAISE_RECORDED = 1
global CORAL_PRAISE_COUNT = 9 `how many phrases there are. the files are praise-1 to praise-9.
global dim CORAL_PRAISE_SFX(16)
global dim CORAL_PRAISE_HEARD(16) `1 for every phrase that has had its turn, this time around
global CORAL_PRAISE_LAST = -1 `the one that played last
global CORAL_PRAISE_VOLUME# = .55 `how loud a phrase is, from 0 to 1
global CORAL_PRAISE_HOLD = 8000 `the chord stays, and no other melody starts, for this many milliseconds after one

` CORAL. the hidden game. nothing in the game says that it is there.
` coral belongs to a place on the board, and not to the fish that is in it. a fish that is swapped off of
` coral leaves the coral behind.
` a few patterns are out at any time. a pattern is a list of steps for the cursor, like up, left, up. when the
` last steps that the cursor took on its own are exactly a pattern, walked from either end (left, left, up
` is the same pattern as down, right, right), and the very next thing is a swap that
` pops something, the pattern is cast: coral grows on every cell that the cursor walked over, and the pattern
` is used up. a new one takes its place, and they get longer as more of them are cast. a pattern that was
` cast is not given out again, until CORAL_USED_MAX more have been cast after it. see THE SONGS, further down.
` anything but a step of the cursor forgets the steps so far. a step that the cursor could not take is not a step.
` THE FISH ON CORAL SING. a fish that is on coral, and is well, never pops. when it is part of a group that
` is big enough, the fish of the group that are over open water pop, and the ones on coral stay where they
` are and sing: each one swells, a heart comes up from it, the coral under it lights up,
` and it scores CORAL_SCORE_BONUS times what it would have
` scored by popping. a stack behind it scores too, and stays.
` a group of fish that have all sung does not sing again, so it does not score every time that the
` board is looked at. it sings again when a fish that has not sung joins it: one that a swap brought,
` or one that fell in. CORAL_SUNG is how that is known. having sung goes with the fish when a swap
` moves it, for as long as it stays on coral that is well, so fish that have sung can not be moved
` around on the coral to sing over and over. swapCellStacks, in fish_funcs, takes it along.
` a group of fish that have all sung already is not a group at all, as far as the songs are concerned.
` when a swap puts one together, a heart comes up from each of its fish, and the multiplier lets the
` swap off, the same as it does for CORAL_HELD_GROUPS. it scores nothing.
` a fish that is belly up, or a skeleton, pops on coral like anywhere else. so does every fish on horror
` coral. and a pop that is forced takes the fish on coral with it: a stingray's column, the bomb, the
` waste bucket, a bottle that pays out. see FORCED POPS. coralHolds, in fish_routines_coral, is the rule.
` coral used to make a group need to be CORAL_GROUP_EXTRA bigger to pop. that is 0 now, which turns it off.
` the water of the whole screen wobbles all of the time, and it wobbles less over coral. see ScreenEffect.fx.
` all of this is the same in a zen game. there the cursor goes around the edges of the board, and a step
` around an edge is a step like any other.
` the routines are in fish_routines_coral.
global CORAL_SWAP_A = -1 `the two cells of the last swap that the multiplier has yet to hear about, or -1
global CORAL_SWAP_B = -1
global CORAL_HELD_GROUPS = 0 `how many groups that swap put together that only coral kept from popping. handle_combos counts them.
global dim CORAL_CELLS(400) `1 for every cell of the board that has coral on it

` HORROR CORAL. a song that is sung with the dead brings coral that is diseased: when the cursor passes
` over a belly up fish anywhere on the way along a pattern, or any of the fish that the swap at the end
` of the pattern pops is belly up, the coral that the pattern grows is horror coral. it is, too, when a
` dead fish is on any cell of the pattern as the swap is judged: one that died after the cursor had
` passed it, or the one that the swap brought onto the last cell. a skeleton is as dead as a belly up fish.
` every song says which kind the coral on its cells is from then on, so a song with no dead fish in it
` heals the horror coral that it crosses, and a song with one spoils the coral that it crosses.
` it is dark and sickly, with tentacles, eyes and skulls coming out of it in place of the things that grow
` on coral. see CoralEffect.fx. it counts as coral in every other way, but no fish is happy on it.
global dim CORAL_SICK(400) `1 for every cell whose coral is horror coral
global CORAL_SICK_COUNT = 0 `how many cells have horror coral on them
global dim CORAL_TRAIL_DEAD(8) `1 for every step of the cursor that went from, or came to, a belly up fish. see CORAL_TRAIL_DIR.
global CORAL_PENDING_SICK = 0 `1 if the pattern that is waiting on a swap was walked over a belly up fish
global INFO_INDEX_CORAL_SICK = -1 `the spot that info mode has for it, once there is some. see coralPlaceInfo.

` THE TWO ENDINGS. the hidden game can be won, and it can be lost, and either one ends the game there and then,
` whatever round it is. the score is the same as for any game: how many moves it took.
`   the reef is saved     when CORAL_END_SHARE cells in a hundred have coral on them that is not horror
`                         coral. the fish leave their cells and
`                         swim around among the coral in schools, every kind of fish together, slowly,
`                         and hearts come up from them.
`   the ancient one       when that many of the cells of the board have horror coral on them. the water
`                         goes bad: the edges of the screen close in, dark and green, and the bottom of it
`                         goes thick, with bubbles coming up. the sea behind the board fades away, and what
`                         is there in place of it is stars, seen through a black hole. tentacles come up,
`                         a few here and one there, with waits in between. and the fish die where they
`                         are: one after another, each turns into a skeleton. this one wins, if a board is ever both.
` coralRecount sees that one is due. handle_coral_ending, which the main loop comes to on every frame, waits
` for the board to settle, plays it, and then asks for the end of the game, which is the same end as always:
` the initials, and the table of high scores. the row of a game that ended one of these ways has a badge
` on it. see HISCORE_BADGE_STEP.
` THE BARS OF THE CORAL. two little bars under the songs say how close each ending is. the bar of the
` reef is there as soon as there is any coral, and the bar of the ancient one as soon as there is any
` horror coral. that one is green, with slime and tentacles on it. each is full when its ending is due,
` and a full bar fizzes until the board has settled and the ending starts. see fish_routines_coralbars.
global CORAL_END_SHARE = 60 `how many cells in a hundred need the one kind of coral for its ending
global CORAL_BAR_X = 32 `where the bars go. they line up with the songs, and are as wide as the songs are.
global CORAL_BAR_WIDTH = 64
global CORAL_BAR_GAP = 3 `how far under the songs the first one is, in pixels
global CORAL_BAR_STEP = 9 `and how far under that one the second one is
global dim CORAL_BAR_BACKS(2)
global dim CORAL_BAR_FILLS(2)
global dim CORAL_BAR_SHOWN#(2) `how full each one is drawn, from 0 to 1. it chases the real thing.
global CORAL_BAR_DRIP_COUNT = 6 `how many drips of slime the bar of the ancient one has
global CORAL_BAR_WISP_COUNT = 3 `and how many tentacles
global CORAL_BAR_WISP_TALL = 3 `how many pixels tall a tentacle is
global dim CORAL_BAR_DRIPS(6)
global dim CORAL_BAR_WISPS(9)
global CORAL_BAR_FIZZ_MS = 0 `when a full bar sends up its next bubbles

#constant CORAL_ENDING_REEF 1
#constant CORAL_ENDING_ANCIENT 2
global CORAL_ENDING = 0 `which of them the game that is being played ended with, or 0
global CORAL_ENDING_DUE = 0 `which of them is waiting for the board to settle, or 0
global CORAL_END_SWELL_MS = 2500 `how long the fish take to get going, in milliseconds

` THE SCHOOLS. when the reef is saved, every fish sets off on its own, whichever way it likes, and wanders.
` after a little while the fish start to find their own kind, and before long every kind is a school.
` a school has a spot that it follows, which goes around the board in a slow loop of its own, and every
` fish of the school has a place around that spot, in a spiral, with the first fish in the middle. a fish
` does not jump to its place. it turns toward it, a little more on every frame, so the school strings
` out behind the spot when it turns.
global CORAL_DANCE_SCATTER# = 24 `how fast a fish swims while it is on its own, in pixels a second
global CORAL_DANCE_SCATTER_MS = 2400 `how long the fish are on their own before they start to school, in milliseconds
global CORAL_DANCE_GATHER_MS = 5500 `and how long it takes from then until they are schooling as much as they ever do
global CORAL_DANCE_CHASE# = 44 `the fastest that a fish swims to get to its place in its school, in pixels a second
global CORAL_DANCE_TURN# = .07 `how much of the way a fish turns toward its place on a frame, once it is schooling
global dim CORAL_DANCE_VX#(400) `which way every fish is swimming, and how fast, in pixels a second
global dim CORAL_DANCE_VY#(400)
global CORAL_DANCE_SPEED# = .55 `how fast the schools go around. 1 is a loop of the board in about 18 seconds.
global CORAL_DANCE_SPACING# = 8.5 `how far apart the fish of a school are, in pixels
global CORAL_DANCE_ROAM# = .40 `how far from the middle of the board a school goes, as a share of the board
global dim CORAL_DANCE_X#(400) `where every fish is, in pixels of the game
global dim CORAL_DANCE_Y#(400)
global dim CORAL_DANCE_SLOT(400) `which place in its school every fish has, counting from 0 in the middle
global CORAL_END_RISE_MS = 12000 `how long the tentacles take to come all the way up
global CORAL_END_CURSOR_MS = 700 `how long the cursor takes to fade away at the start of an ending
` HOW MUCH THE SCREEN SHAKES. an amount is in pixels of the game's own screen, whatever the size of the
` window: the screen moves up to half of it in any direction. see "set screen shake amount".
global SHAKE_POP_AMOUNT# = 6 `the knock that a fish gives the screen when it pops. it is over in a moment.
global SHAKE_FREE_MOVE_AMOUNT# = 3 `the smaller knock of every move that the "free moves" card gives back
global CORAL_END_SHAKE_FROM# = .5 `how much the screen shakes when the ancient one starts to come up
global CORAL_END_SHAKE_TO# = 4 `and how much once the tentacles are all the way up. it grows steadily from the one to the other, and stays.
global CORAL_END_DIM = 70 `how dark the screen gets behind the tentacles, out of 255. the stars have to show through it.
global SEABED_VOID# = 0 `how much of the sea behind the board has turned into the stars, from 0 to 1. see SeabedEffect.fx.
global CORAL_END_WORDS_MS = 900 `how long the words take to fade in
global CORAL_END_HOLD_MS = 2600 `how long it is, once the words are there, before the player can move on
global CORAL_END_MAX_MS = 14000 `and how long before it moves on by itself
global CORAL_END_TEXT_ID `the words
global CORAL_END_SUB_TEXT_ID `and the smaller words under them
global CORAL_END_DIM_SPRITE `darkens the screen behind the tentacles
global TENTACLE_FX_ID
global TENTACLE_SPRITE `the tentacles. see TentacleEffect.fx.
global ORDER_END_DIM = 28 `on top of the fish, the bubbles and the specks
global ORDER_TENTACLES = 29 `and on top of that, and underneath the score text

` the sounds of the two endings. the reef gets a fanfare: a run of notes up a chord on the bell, and then the
` chord, with the choir under it.
` the ancient one gets the sound of the deep, gone wrong. there is no tune in it. the music of the game fades
` away, all but the rumble and the low hum of the deep, which come up. over those:
`   a drone      three notes of the swell that are right next to each other, played an octave down, and one
`                of them a little flat, so that they grind against each other
`   a sonar      the ping, an octave down, slow, like something is being looked for
`   something    the low whale, as far down as it goes, now and then. it is very big.
`   wails        the voices in pain, far off: quiet, and anywhere from a little low to an octave low, each
`                one somewhere else from side to side. there are more of them as the tentacles come up.
`   screams      the slow screams, one laid over another. a new one starts every few seconds, well
`                before the one before it is over, and they take turns, so that there are always two or
`                three of them going at once, each one somewhere else and a little lower or higher.
` see THE MUSIC, further up, for the instruments and the shots.
global CORAL_FANFARE_VOLUME# = .75 `how loud a note of the fanfare is, from 0 to 1
global CORAL_DEEP_DRONE_MS = 4200 `how long it is between one breath of the drone and the next, in milliseconds
global CORAL_DEEP_DRONE# = .85 `how loud the drone is, from 0 to 1
global CORAL_DEEP_FLAT# = .045 `how flat the one note of the drone is, in octaves. a twelfth of an octave is the next note down.
global CORAL_DEEP_PING_MS = 4700 `how long it is between one ping of the sonar and the next
global CORAL_DEEP_PING# = .5 `how loud the sonar is, next to how loud it usually is
global CORAL_DEEP_WHALE_MS = 6500 `about how long it is between one groan of the whale and the next
global CORAL_DEEP_WHALE# = 1.3 `how loud the whale is, next to how loud it usually is
global CORAL_DEEP_WAIL_SLOW = 2300 `about how long it is between one wail and the next when the tentacles start to come up
global CORAL_DEEP_WAIL_FAST = 800 `and when they are all the way up
global CORAL_DEEP_WAIL# = .30 `how loud a wail is, at the most, from 0 to 1. they are far off.
global CORAL_DEEP_SCREAM_MS = 4000 `about how long it is between one slow scream and the next. each one lasts a lot longer than that.
global CORAL_DEEP_SCREAM# = .55 `how loud a slow scream is, at the most, next to how loud a scream usually is
global CORAL_DEEP_SCREAM_LOW# = .25 `how much lower one can be played than it is, in octaves. lower is slower too.

` THE MAIN MENU REMEMBERS. once a game has ended with the reef saved, there is a reef in the sea on the left
` of the main menu, from then on: under the water, with its tops standing out of it, and a few of the fish
` of the game swimming over it. it fades away as the camera goes under at the start of a game.
` and once a game has ended with the ancient one, something
` is out on the horizon, a long way off, coming slowly toward the boat. it starts over every time that
` the main menu comes up, and it never gets there. both can be there at once. see SurfaceEffect.fx.
` the FISH_MENU_REEF and FISH_MENU_ANCIENT environment variables put them there, for working on the game.
#constant PREF_ENDING_REEF 8
#constant PREF_ENDING_ANCIENT 9
` the player can turn either of them off again, on the options page, once it is there. the lines for
` them are not on the page until then. frame_surface, in fish_routines_surface, is what listens.
#constant PREF_MENU_REEF 10
#constant PREF_MENU_MONSTER 11
global OPTION_MENU_REEF = 1 `1 while the reef is wanted on the main menu
global OPTION_MENU_MONSTER = 1 `and 1 while the ancient one is
global SURFACE_REEF = 0 `1 once the reef has been saved in some game
global SURFACE_FISH_COUNT = 7 `how many fish swim over the reef. there is one of every kind that a game starts with.
global dim SURFACE_FISH_SPRITES(8)
global dim SURFACE_FISH_GO#(8) `how far across the reef each one is, from 0 on the left to 1 on the right
global SURFACE_FISH_MS = 0 `the last frame that they were moved on
global SURFACE_FISH_LAP# = 22 `how many seconds the slowest of them takes to get across. each of the others is a little quicker than the one before.
global SURFACE_FISH_SIZE = 16 `how big one of them is, in pixels. this is the size of their pictures, and any smaller leaves pixels out of them.
global SURFACE_REEF_X = 96 `where the middle of the reef is: how far across the screen,
global SURFACE_REEF_DOWN = 78 `and how far down from the horizon the fish are, which is a little under the waterline of the reef

` THE REEFS OF THE MAIN MENU. there are two: one on the left, a way off, and one on the right, in front of the
` boat, which is closer, so it is bigger and further down the screen, and it is the other way around.
` each is a sprite of its own that covers the screen, with a copy of the reef effect of its own. see ReefEffect.fx.
` for each: how far across the screen the middle of it is, how far down from the horizon its waterline is,
` how many pixels one unit of its shape is, whether it is the other way around (-1) or not (1), and a
` number of its own, which is what makes its lumps different from the other one's.
global dim SURFACE_REEF_FX(2)
global dim SURFACE_REEF_SPRITES(2)
global dim SURFACE_REEF_AT_X(2)
global dim SURFACE_REEF_AT_DOWN(2)
global dim SURFACE_REEF_AT_SCALE(2)
global dim SURFACE_REEF_AT_FLIP(2)
global dim SURFACE_REEF_SEED#(2)
global SURFACE_REEF2_X = 452 `the middle of the reef on the right, for the fish that swim around it,
global SURFACE_REEF2_DOWN = 118 `and how far down from the horizon they are

` the crab and the worm do not swim. each of them is on the rock of a reef that stands out of the water,
` going slowly back and forth: the crab on the reef on the left, and the worm on the one on the right.
` these are where on the screen the middle of each one's walk is, across, and down from the horizon,
` and how far to either side it goes. they have to sit on the knuckles of rock in SurfaceEffect.fx.
global SURFACE_CRAB_X = 70
global SURFACE_CRAB_DOWN = 43
global SURFACE_WORM_X = 487
global SURFACE_WORM_DOWN = 74
global SURFACE_WALK = 7

global SURFACE_REEF_FADE# = 3 `how fast the reef fades as the camera goes under. 3 has it gone a third of the way down.
global SURFACE_ANCIENT = 0 `1 once the ancient one has been summoned in some game
global SURFACE_MONSTER_START_MS = 0 `when it set out
global SURFACE_MONSTER_SEEN_MS = 0 `the last frame that the main menu was drawn, to tell when it has come up again
global SURFACE_MONSTER_HALF# = 70 `how many seconds it takes to come half of the way that it ever comes

` WHAT HORROR CORAL DOES TO THE FISH. every time that a move has been made and the board has settled, each
` fish that is on horror coral has a chance to go belly up, and each fish that is on horror coral and
` belly up already has a chance to turn into a skeleton. a bottle never goes belly up, so one that is on
` horror coral has that second chance right away. a skeleton stays a skeleton, and never goes belly up again.
` the fish under the cursor does not go belly up, because the cursor has to come to a fish to pop it.
` rollHorrorCoral, in fish_routines_coral, does it.
global HORROR_BELLY_CHANCE = 30 `how many of those fish in a hundred go belly up
global HORROR_SKELETON_CHANCE = 30 `and how many of the belly up ones in a hundred turn into skeletons

` SKELETONS. a skeleton is a fish whose bellyUp is 2. it is still the kind of fish that it was, and it
` is that kind in every way: it matches, pops, scores, stacks, stings, shoots and pays out as that kind,
` and whatever makes a copy of a fish makes a copy of a skeleton. it is drawn as the bones of the fish,
` the right way up.
` it is not belly up any more: it does not pop when the cursor comes to it, and a skeleton stingray
` shoots up. but the waste bucket takes skeletons along with the belly up fish, and a skeleton is as
` dead as a belly up fish is, when a song is sung. see HORROR CORAL.
` and it is a wildcard. the group of a fish that a swap has just moved can go on through skeletons of
` any kind, for the look at the board that comes right after that swap. so worm, worm, skeleton, worm
` is only a pair and a worm until a worm, or a skeleton, is swapped onto the end of it, and then it is
` five. a skeleton that is swapped makes a group of whichever kind makes the biggest one.
` a group that no swap has just touched does not go through skeletons, so nothing pops on its own
` because of one. skeletonsBeforeCombos and skeletonsAfterCombos, in fish_routines_coral, do that,
` around the search for groups in handle_combos. see EVERY SKELETON IS THE SAME PILE OF BONES, further down.
` a bottle that is a skeleton pays out BOTTLE_SKELETON_GAIN when it gets to the bottom, in place of BOTTLE_MULT_GAIN.
global BOTTLE_SKELETON_GAIN = 2

` EVERY SKELETON IS THE SAME PILE OF BONES, whatever kind of fish it was. it is not bound to what it
` used to be. SKELETON_TEX still has a picture for every kind, but they are all TEX_BONES.
global TEX_BONES `the pile of bones
global TEX_GHOST `the ghost, which is white, so that it can be drawn in the color of any fish

` THE GHOST OF A SKELETON. when a skeleton is part of a group of fish that matched, nothing about a
` pile of bones says what it matched as, and a skeleton that a swap brought in can stand for any kind.
` so before the group pops, the skeleton pops into a ghost: the bones are gone, with a puff of
` bubbles, and there is a ghost in their place, in the color of the kind of fish that the group is.
` every ghost is the same ghost, and only the color is different. it stays for SKELETON_GHOST_MS, and
` then it pops with the rest of the group, the way that any fish does. the pops of that group, and of
` every group after it, wait for that long, so all of it can be seen. a pop that is forced, like the
` waste bucket, matches nothing, and has no ghosts. handle_combos asks for them, and the routines are
` in fish_routines_coral, with the rest of the skeletons.
global SKELETON_GHOST_MS = 800 `how long the ghost is there before the pop, in milliseconds
global SKELETON_GHOST_POP = 200 `how long the ghost takes to settle to its size, when it pops out of the bones
global SKELETON_GHOST_MAX = 48 `how many there can be at once
global dim SKELETON_GHOST_SPRITES(48)
global dim SKELETON_GHOST_CELL(48) `the cell that each one is over
global dim SKELETON_GHOST_FROM(48) `when each one pops out of its bones
global dim SKELETON_GHOST_UNTIL(48) `and when it goes, which is when its skeleton pops. 0 for one that is not in use.
global dim SKELETON_AS(400) `the kind of fish that the skeleton in every cell stood for, the last time that the board was searched for groups, or 0
global dim SKELETON_TEX(17) `the picture of the skeleton of every kind of fish
global dim SKELETON_SHOWN(400) `1 for every cell whose fish is being drawn as a skeleton. frame_fish_fx keeps it.
global dim SKELETON_KIND(400) `while handle_combos searches for groups, the kind of fish that a skeleton that a group has taken really is, or 0
global SKELETON_WILD_A = -1 `the two cells of the swap that was just made, or -1. the groups of the fish in them can go through skeletons.
global SKELETON_WILD_B = -1

global dim CORAL_SHOWN#(400) `how much of the coral in every cell is drawn. it chases the real thing, so that new coral grows in.

` HOW THE CORAL IS DRAWN. it is rock, with tufts of coral growing out of it, and all of it is drawn by the
` coral effect. see CoralEffect.fx. the game only says where the coral is: there is a white square for every
` cell that has coral, as see-through as the coral in the cell is grown, and they are all drawn to a render
` target of their own. one sprite that covers the screen, underneath the fish, has that render target for
` its picture and the coral effect on it, and the effect turns the squares into the rock.
global dim CORAL_SPRITES(400) `the squares
global CORAL_SPRITE_COUNT = 0
global TARGET_CORAL
global CORAL_FX_ID
global CORAL_VIEW_SPRITE

` the fish cast shadows on the coral. every fish on the board is drawn a second time, as a plain picture of
` itself right where the fish is, to another render target. the coral effect looks in that for the fish,
` and darkens the coral a little way down and to the right of every one. nothing else ever sees them.
global TARGET_CORAL_SHADOW
global dim CORAL_SHADOW_SPRITES(400)
global CORAL_SHADOWS_ON = 0 `1 while the shadows are being kept up, which is only while there is coral
` stepCoral runs on every frame, so it keeps track of what there is nothing to do for.
global dim CORAL_DRAWN(400) `1 for every cell whose square of coral is showing
global CORAL_DRAW_IDLE = 0 `1 while there is no coral, and every square has been put away, so the squares are not looked at
global CORAL_SING_WAITING = 0 `1 while some fish has yet to swell. see CORAL_SING_DUE.
global dim CORAL_SHADOW_DRAWN(400) `1 for every cell whose fish has its shadow showing
global dim CORAL_NEAR(400) `1 for every cell that has coral, or is right next to some. only the fish of these have shadows. coralRecount works it out.

` A FISH ON CORAL sits down on it. it is drawn a little smaller than a fish that is over open water, and it
` shrinks down to that as it gets there. frame_scale_wave, in fish_routines, sizes the fish.
global CORAL_SIT_SHRINK# = .125 `how much smaller, where 1 is all the way to nothing. this takes a fish from 16 pixels to 14.
global dim CORAL_SIT#(400) `how big the fish in every cell is drawn, where 1 is full size. it chases where it should be.

` and it is happy there. when a fish comes to sit on coral, a heart may float up from it, and now and then
` one floats up from a fish that has been sitting for a while. spawnHeart, in fish_routines_bubbles, makes one.
global CORAL_HEART_CHANCE = 60 `how many fish in a hundred send up a heart when they sit down
global CORAL_HEART_GAP_MIN = 900 `after a heart from a fish that was already sitting, the next one comes
global CORAL_HEART_GAP_MAX = 2700 `somewhere between these, in milliseconds
global CORAL_HEART_NEXT_MS = 0 `when that next one is due
global dim CORAL_SAT_VALUE(400) `the kind of fish that was sitting on the coral of every cell on the last frame, or 0
global CORAL_COUNT = 0 `how many cells have coral on them
