` DIAGONALS. the other hidden thing. nothing in the game says that it is there.
` fish that only touch at the corners are not a group, and they never pop. but when a swap leaves one of
` the two fish that it moved in a line of DIAGONAL_LENGTH or more fish of its own kind, corner to corner,
` the line pays out: every fish of it picks up one more fish for its stack, and scores DIAGONAL_POINTS
` times the multiplier, one after another along the line. then the multiplier goes up, and a swap that
` popped nothing is not held against the player.
` a line of DIAGONAL_LENGTH adds DIAGONAL_MULT_GAIN to the multiplier, and every fish more than that adds
` it again: a line of four adds one, a line of five adds two, and a line of six adds three.
` a line whose fish have all paid out already does not pay again. DIAGONAL_PAID is how that is known,
` and it goes with the fish wherever the fish goes, the same as its stack does. so a fish that is
` swapped out of a line and back in gets nothing the second time: the bubbles come up along the line,
` and the swap is not held against the player, and that is all. the same goes for a line that fell
` apart and was put back together out of the same fish. but one fish that has not paid out is enough
` to make the whole line good again: every fish of it is paid, and the multiplier gets all that a
` line of that length is worth.
` the line comes before the groups. when the same swap makes a line and a group that pops, the line
` pays out first: the fish have their new stacks when they pop, the group is scored with the
` multiplier that the line raised, and the pops wait until the line has been seen.
` it takes swaps that pop nothing to put a line together, and those cost the multiplier, so the line
` gives back about what it took. the stacks are what is left over.
` the swap has to make the line. a line that was there already pays nothing, and neither does trading
` two fish of the same kind. a "free move" and the robot do not make lines, the same as they do not
` touch the multiplier. the first dive has no lines, until it has a multiplier.
` the routines are in fish_routines_diagonal.
` two cards change it, and neither one says what it is about. they call a line "the crooked bar", and a
` player who has not found the lines is left to wonder what that is.
`   "sneaking death", a passive: the fish of a line that pays out do not pick up a fish for their stacks.
`       they go belly up, and one that is belly up already turns into a skeleton. they still score, and
`       the multiplier still goes up. a skeleton is not part of any line, so that fish is done with lines.
`   "lower the bar", a passive: a line takes one fish less. getDiagonalLength, in fish_routines_diagonal,
`       says how many it takes, and the multiplier counts from there: three adds one, four adds two.
global DIAGONAL_LENGTH = 4 `how many fish it takes, without the "lower the bar" card
global DIAGONAL_POINTS = 1 `how much every fish of the line scores, before the multiplier
global DIAGONAL_MULT_GAIN = 1 `how much a line adds to the multiplier, and how much more for every fish past DIAGONAL_LENGTH
global DIAGONAL_STEP_MS = 90 `how long after one fish of the line pays out the next one does, in milliseconds
global DIAGONAL_LINES = 0 `how many lines the swap that is being judged made, paid or not. judgeDiagonals counts them, and judgeMultiplierSwaps uses them up.
global DIAGONAL_GAIN = 0 `and how much those lines add to the multiplier. judgeDiagonals adds it, and this is 0 again.
global DIAGONAL_WAIT = 0 `how long the pops of the same swap are held back for a line that paid out, in milliseconds
global DIAGONAL_AFTER_MS = 250 `how long it is from the last fish of a line paying out to the first pop
global dim DIAGONAL_PAID(400) `the kind of fish that has paid out in every cell, and is still the fish that is there, or 0
global dim DIAGONAL_LINE(32) `the cells of the line that was looked at last, from its left end to its right
global DIAGONAL_LINE_COUNT = 0

` THE HINTS OF THE DIAGONALS. they ride on the current. when a wave gets to a fish that has one of its own
` kind at a corner, the fish does not lean into the wave like the rest. it leans toward the other one, and
` the other one leans back. there is nearly always a pair like that somewhere, so it is easy to take for
` nothing. and the fish of a line that is one short of paying out shuffle: when the wave gets to the
` left end of the line, the fish there drifts toward the next one and back, and then the next one
` does, and so on down the line. the last one drifts toward the corner that has no fish in it yet.
` it is slow, and it goes a good way. a push is in whole pixels, so a short one is a hop, which was
` jarring, and a swell in place of it was too faint to see.
global DIAGONAL_HINT_EVERY = 200 `how often the hints are worked out again, in milliseconds
global DIAGONAL_HINT_MS = 0 `when they are next due
global DIAGONAL_HINT_LEAN# = .25 `how far a fish leans toward one corner, in radians
global DIAGONAL_HINT_SHUFFLE# = .12 `how far a fish of a line that is one short drifts, where 1 is the size of a fish
global DIAGONAL_HINT_SHUFFLE_MS = 1800 `how long the drift of one fish takes, there and back, in milliseconds
global DIAGONAL_HINT_STEP_MS = 600 `and how long after one fish starts the next one does
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_KIND(400) `what stepDiagonalHints works with: the kind of every fish that can be part of a line, and 0 for the rest,
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_ALONG(400) `how far along its line every fish is, counted from 1 at the left end,
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_HEAD(400) `the cell at the left end of its line,
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_LEFT(400) `and how many fish of the line are left from it on, counting itself
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_X(400) `which way the fish in every cell leans: toward the right, or the left if it is less than 0,
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_Y(400) `and down, or up
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_SLOPE(400) `for a fish that shuffles, which way its line goes: 1 is down to the right, and -1 is up to the right. 0 for the rest.
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_DELAY(400) `and how long after a wave of the current sets out its nudge starts, in milliseconds
global dim DIAGONAL_HINT_PLACE(400) `and which fish of its line it is, counting from 0 at the left end
global DIAGONAL_HINT_NOW_MS = -100000 `when the debug key last asked to see the shuffle, right away, on every line that has one. see frame_dbg.

` TUNA. a silly secret. typing the word TUNA while the fish are on the screen makes them into a QR code,
` that a phone can read. a pool of light falls on the water, and there is a dark fish in it for every
` dark module of the code. where a module is light there is no fish, only the water. the fish of the
` board each go to a dark module of their own, anywhere in the code, and get smaller. then the rest of
` the dark modules are taken by more fish, that come out of the fish of the board: each one swims out
` of the nearest of them, and is the same kind of fish.
` see TunaFishEffect.fx and ScreenEffect.fx.
` it stays for TUNA_HOLD_MS, or until the cursor moves. then the fish that came out go back into the
` fish that they came out of, and the fish of the board go back to their places.
` the first time that it happens, it gets an entry in the codec. see CODEC_ENTRY_TUNA.
` nothing else moves while the code is up: the fish do not lean or shuffle, and no hint comes.
` the debug keys use T and U, so in debug mode the word does nothing, and Q does it instead.
` there can be more than one code, and then one is picked at random every time. the codes are in
` fish_tuna_code, which build/make-tuna-qr.py writes. where they lead to is set there.
` the routines are in fish_routines_tuna.
global TUNA_HOLD_MS = 30000 `how long the code stays up, in milliseconds
global TUNA_CODE_SIZE = 25 `how many modules wide the code is
global TUNA_QUIET = 2 `how many modules of open, lit water go around it. a phone needs some, to find the edge of the code.
global TUNA_PITCH = 8 `how far apart the fish are, in pixels of the game
global TUNA_ART = 8 `how big a fish is drawn. the pictures are 16 pixels, so this is half the size, and every other pixel of one.
global TUNA_SHADOW = 8 `how big the shadow under a fish is, in pixels of the game. the same as TUNA_PITCH fills the whole module, and anything smaller made the code hard for a phone to read.
global TUNA_MARGIN = 10 `how much further than that the pool of light goes, in pixels of the game, before it starts to fade out
global TUNA_IN_FRAMES = 170 `how long it all takes, in frames. the fish of the board take the first half, and the fish that come out of them the second.
global TUNA_OUT_FRAMES = 100 `and to go away again
global TUNA_FX_ID `the effect of the fish that come out of the fish of the board
global TARGET_TUNA `the picture of where the dark modules are, for the screen effect: white, with a black square for every one
global TUNA_ON = 0 `1 while the code is up, or on its way there
global TUNA_SHOWN# = 0 `how far along it is, from 0 to 1, at a steady pace. the fish that come out go by this.
global TUNA_EASED# = 0 `the same for the fish of the board, and the light. they are done in the first half.
global TUNA_START_MS = 0 `when it started
global TUNA_FROM_X = 0 `where the cursor was then
global TUNA_FROM_Y = 0
global TUNA_TYPED = 0 `how many letters of the word have been typed so far
global dim TUNA_WORD(4) `the letters of the word, where 0 is A
global dim TUNA_KEYS(26) `the key of every letter, from A to Z
global TUNA_CODE_COUNT = 0 `how many codes there are to pick from. each one leads somewhere else.
global TUNA_PICK = 0 `which of them the fish are making. a new one is picked at random every time.
global dim TUNA_CODE(200) `the codes, as fish_tuna_code has them: a number for every row, one code after another. there is room for 8.
global dim TUNA_DARK(5000) `and with a 1 for every dark module, row after row
global dim TUNA_SPRITES(625) `a fish for every module of the code. the ones of the dark modules that no fish of the board took are shown.
global dim TUNA_SHADOWS(625) `a black square for every module of the code. the ones of the dark modules are shown.
global dim TUNA_TAKEN(625) `1 for every module of the code that a fish of the board has taken
global TUNA_DARK_COUNT = 0 `how many dark modules the code that was picked has
global TUNA_EXTRAS_OUT = 0 `1 while the fish that come out are on the screen
global TUNA_EXTRA_COUNT = 0 `how many of them there are
global TUNA_CURSOR_FADED = 0 `1 while the cursor is faded out, to be out of the way of the code
global dim TUNA_CELL_DX(400) `for every cell of the board: how far its fish goes to get to its place in the code, in pixels of the game
global dim TUNA_CELL_DY(400)
global dim TUNA_CELL_AT(400) `and which module of the code that place is
global TUNA_MID_X = 0 `the middle of the code on the screen, in pixels of the game
global TUNA_MID_Y = 0
global TUNA_SHRINK# = 0 `how much smaller the fish of the board are drawn on this frame. 0 is their own size, and -.5 is half of it.

` MORSE. a word can be tapped out in Morse code, on anything that the player can press. so it
` works on a controller, which has no letters. TUNA makes the QR code, the same as typing it does,
` and SOS turns debug mode on, cheats and all, even in a shipped game. the words are set in setup_morse.
`
` THE SIGNALS. a dot is a short push, and a dash is a long one. these are all of them:
`     TUNA    -  ..-  -.  .-        makes the QR code. see tunaAsked, in fish_routines_tuna.
`     SOS     ...  ---  ...         turns debug mode on, and marks the game as one that had it. see morseSos, in fish_routines_morse.
`     CQ      -.-.  --.-            the radio answers: it bips every signal back, but for this one and SOS. see morseReplyStart.
`     SING    ...  ..  -.  --.      plays a hint of the hidden game, the bright way that a group of five does. see morseSing.
`     SONG    ...  ---  -.  --.     the same.
`     CTHULHU -.-.  -  ....  ..-  .-..  ....  ..-     a tentacle of the ancient one comes up for a moment, with a scream, and goes. see morseCthulhu.
` when any of them has been heard, the radio taps back .-. which is R, for "received".
` the codec has an entry for the radio, which is unlocked the first time that the radio comes on, and
` which only gives CQ away. and it has an entry for every signal but SOS and CQ, which is unlocked when
` that signal is sent. SOS is not in the codec at all. see CODEC_ENTRY_RADIO, and MORSE_WORD_CODEC.
` a new one is a line here, a number like MORSE_WORD_SOS below, a morseWord in setup_morse, and
` something that looks for the number in MORSE_HEARD. the letters are in morseAlphabet.
`
` first the radio has to be turned on: one thing is held down, on its own, for MORSE_WAKE_MS. a sign
` sounds when it comes on. from then on every push is a dot or a dash, by how long it is held, and a
` tone sounds for as long as it is held. the last pushes are remembered, and when the end of them is
` the dots and dashes of a word, that word has been heard, whatever came before it. so STUNA is TUNA.
` the quiet after a letter does not have to be any longer than the quiet inside of one.
` when a word has been heard, the radio goes off. it also goes off, with another sign, when nobody
` has pushed anything for MORSE_SLEEP_MS, or when two things are held at once, like a swap: then the
` player has gone back to the game.
` nobody taps at the same speed, so the game keeps an average of how long this player's dots are,
` and of how long their dashes are, and a push is a dash when it is longer than the middle of the two.
` every push moves its own average half way to itself, and pulls the other one along a little, to a
` third or to three times its length, because that is how long they are meant to be.
` now and then a shooting star crosses the sky of the splash screen, somewhere new every time, and its trail is the dots and dashes of CQ. see morseStarsMake.
` Tools/morse_bip_gen.py makes the sounds. the routines are in fish_routines_morse.
global MORSE_WAKE_MS = 2000 `how long one thing has to be held, on its own, to turn the radio on, in milliseconds
global MORSE_SLEEP_MS = 3000 `and how long the radio waits for a push before it goes off again
global MORSE_DOT_START = 100 `how long a dot is taken to be, in milliseconds, before any has been heard
global MORSE_DASH_START = 300 `and a dash
global MORSE_MIDDLE_MIN# = 160 `the middle of the two never goes under this, so that a lot of quick taps do not turn a slower tap into a dash
global MORSE_PUSH_MAX_MS = 1500 `a push that is held for longer than this is not a dot or a dash
global MORSE_VOLUME# = .3 `how loud the tone is. the same as VOLUME in Tools/morse_bip_gen.py.
global MORSE_SIGN_VOLUME# = .35 `how loud the two signs are. the same as SIGN_VOLUME there.
global MORSE_WORD_ROOM = 8 `how many words there can be
global MORSE_MARK_ROOM = 32 `and how many pushes long a word can be. the last this many pushes are remembered.
global MORSE_WORD_TUNA = 0 `the word that makes the QR code
global MORSE_WORD_SOS = 1 `and the word that turns debug mode on
global MORSE_WORD_CQ = 2 `the word that the radio answers
global MORSE_WORD_SING = 3 `and the two words that play a hint of the hidden game
global MORSE_WORD_SONG = 4
global MORSE_WORD_CTHULHU = 5 `the word that a tentacle comes up for
global CODEC_ENTRY_RADIO = -1 `the entry of the radio in the codec, once setup_codec has run. it is unlocked when the radio first comes on.
global CODEC_ENTRY_SING = -1 `and the entries of the signals, each of which is unlocked when it is sent. TUNA has CODEC_ENTRY_TUNA.
global CODEC_ENTRY_SONG = -1
global CODEC_ENTRY_CTHULHU = -1
global TEX_CODEC_RADIO `and the picture that it has. Tools/radio_icon_gen.py draws it.
global MORSE_REPLY_LEAD = 3 `how long the radio waits before it taps anything back, as so many dots
global MORSE_TENTACLE_LIFE_MS = 5200 `how long the tentacle of CTHULHU is there, from when it starts to come up until it is gone again
global MORSE_TENTACLE_REACH# = .62 `how far up it comes, where 1 is as far as it comes in the ending
global MORSE_TENTACLE_ON = 0 `1 while it is there
global MORSE_TENTACLE_MS = 0 `when it started to come up
global MORSE_TENTACLE_UP# = 0 `how far up it is right now, from 0 to 1
global MORSE_CTHULHU_COUNT = 0 `how many times CTHULHU has been heard
global dim MORSE_WORD_CODEC(8) `the entry of the codec that every word unlocks when it is sent, or -1 for none
global dim MORSE_WORD_NAME$(8) `the letters of every word, for the codec
global MORSE_SPELL_ROOM = 48 `how many numbers long the spelling of a word can be: its pushes, and a 2 after every letter
global MORSE_REPLY_UNIT_MS = 100 `how long a dot is when the radio answers, in milliseconds
global MORSE_REPLY_WAIT = 7 `how long the radio waits between one word and the next when it answers, as so many dots
global MORSE_REPLY_PITCH# = .35 `how much higher the tone is when it is the radio that is tapping, so that it does not sound like the player
global MORSE_MODE = 0 `1 while the radio is on
global MORSE_WAKING = 0 `1 for the rest of the hold that turned the radio on. it is not a dot or a dash.
global MORSE_WOKE = 0 `1 on the frame that the radio came on,
global MORSE_SLEPT = 0 `and on the frame that it went off, because nobody pushed anything, or the player went back to the game
global MORSE_DOT# = 100 `the average length of this player's dots, in milliseconds,
global MORSE_DASH# = 300 `and of their dashes,
global MORSE_MIDDLE# = 200 `and the middle of the two. a push that is longer than this is a dash.
global MORSE_HELD = 0 `1 while something is held down
global MORSE_HELD_MS = 0 `when it went down
global MORSE_FREE_MS = 0 `and when the last push ended
global MORSE_SPOILED = 0 `1 when more than one thing was held during the push, so that it does not count
global MORSE_MARK = -1 `the last push that was heard: 0 for a dot, and 1 for a dash
global MORSE_HEARD = -1 `the word that was finished on this frame, or -1
global MORSE_SOUND# = 0 `how loud the tone is right now
global MORSE_WORD_COUNT = 0 `how many words there are
global MORSE_SOS_COUNT = 0 `how many times SOS has been heard
global MORSE_SING_COUNT = 0 `and SING, or SONG
global dim MORSE_WORD_SECRET(8) `1 for a word that the radio does not give away when it answers
global dim MORSE_WORD_SPELL(384) `the spelling of every word, as morseSpell leaves it. each word has MORSE_SPELL_ROOM numbers.
global dim MORSE_WORD_SPELL_LENGTH(8) `and how many numbers long each one is
global dim MORSE_REPLY(1024) `the answer of the radio: a 1 for every dot of time that the tone sounds, and a 0 for every one that it does not
global MORSE_REPLY_LENGTH = 0 `how many of those there are
global MORSE_REPLY_ON = 0 `1 while the radio is answering
global MORSE_REPLY_AT = 0 `how many dots of time into the answer it is. less than 0 while it waits to start.
global MORSE_REPLY_CLOCK = 0 `the milliseconds that have gone by since the last whole dot
global MORSE_REPLY_LAST_MS = 0 `when the answer was last moved along
global MORSE_REPLY_LIT = 0 `1 while the tone of the answer sounds
global MORSE_PITCH# = 0 `how high the tone is right now
global dim MORSE_CODES(26) `the pushes of every letter, from A to Z: a 1, and then, in binary, a 0 for every dot and a 1 for every dash
global dim MORSE_WORD_MARKS(256) `the pushes of every word: 0 for a dot, and 1 for a dash. each word has MORSE_MARK_ROOM of them.
global dim MORSE_WORD_LENGTH(8) `how many pushes long every word is
global dim MORSE_BUFFER(32) `the last pushes that were heard, the oldest first
global MORSE_BUFFER_COUNT = 0 `and how many of them there are
global dim MORSE_SPELL(64) `the pushes of the word that morseSpell was last asked for, with a 2 after every letter
global MORSE_SPELL_COUNT = 0 `and how many numbers that is
global MORSE_STAR_UNIT_MS = 70 `how long the shooting star of the splash screen takes to cross one step of its path, in milliseconds
global MORSE_STAR_STEP = 3 `how many pixels across a step is. it is one pixel down.
global MORSE_STAR_ODDS = 3 `the star only comes on one crossing in this many. the rest of the time the sky stays empty.
global MORSE_STAR_EDGE = 14 `how far the path keeps from the edges of the splash screen, in pixels
global MORSE_STAR_X = 96 `where on the splash screen the path of this crossing starts. it is somewhere else every time: see morseStarRoll.
global MORSE_STAR_Y = 44
global MORSE_STAR_SEEN = 0 `1 when the star comes on this crossing
global MORSE_STAR_CROSSING = -1 `which crossing this is, counting from 0, or -1 before the first
global MORSE_STAR_LETTER_GAP = 4 `how many steps of nothing there are after a letter, on top of the one that there is after every dot and dash
global MORSE_STAR_FADE = 16 `how long a piece of trail takes to fade away behind the star, as so many steps
global MORSE_STAR_REST = 34 `how long the sky is empty after a crossing, before the next one, the same way
global MORSE_STAR_WAIT = 16 `and how long it is before the first crossing, while the splash screen fades in
global MORSE_STAR_PATH = 0 `how many steps long the path is
global MORSE_STAR_COUNT = 0 `how many of the steps have trail on them
global MORSE_STAR_HEAD = 0 `the star itself
global dim MORSE_STARS(64) `a sprite for every step that has trail on it,
global dim MORSE_STAR_AT_STEP(64) `and which step of the path each one is
global dim MORSE_GLINT(256) `a 1 for every step of the path that has trail on it, and a 0 for every one that does not
global MORSE_STAR_GOING = 0 `1 while the shooting star is being shown
global MORSE_STAR_AT = 0 `how many steps of time have gone by since it first set out. less than 0 while it waits.
global MORSE_STAR_CLOCK = 0 `the milliseconds that have gone by since the last whole step
global MORSE_STAR_LAST_MS = 0 `when it was last moved along
global SFX_CLIP_MORSE `the tone
global SFX_MORSE = 0
global SFX_CLIP_MORSE_ON `the sign that the radio is on
global SFX_MORSE_ON = 0
global SFX_CLIP_MORSE_OFF `and that it has gone off
